OCGRAPH 1
# A PLAYABLE CHARACTER WITH NO C# ANYWHERE. This project contains not one .cs file; everything below
# is the whole of it.
#
# Parenting to `Character` is what makes it work, and that row only became reachable from content in
# 6d7ff99. Before that, `Character` was not a declared class at all -- AverCharacter was abstract, no
# concrete row existed, and the only way to obtain a spawnable character was for a PROJECT to write
# its own C# subclass. That one file was the last thing standing between a project and being entirely
# graph-driven.
#
# WHAT PARENTING GIVES US, and what it does not. The instance gets a real managed AverCharacter
# behind it -- the capsule, the view entity, the yaw/pitch state with its clamp -- which is what
# CharacterMove reaches through. It does NOT get any behaviour from a parent GRAPH class, because
# graph classes inherit native registry flags and kind bits, never another graph's nodes. There is no
# Blueprint-style logic inheritance here and this file does not pretend otherwise.
NAME Player
DESCRIPTION A character driven entirely from a graph: polled input into CharacterMove, no C# at all.

CLASS AN_Player Character

# The three names an event-driven host will supply. Asking for anything else is refused BY NAME at
# load, which is a good error to meet early rather than a mystery at runtime.
PARAM entity int
PARAM deltaTime float

NODE ent Param param=entity
NODE dt Param param=deltaTime

# Look sensitivity, degrees per unit of mouse delta. Separate from move because a mouse delta and a
# key axis are different units and pretending otherwise is how a controller ends up feeling wrong.
NODE look_scale ConstFloat
PIN look_scale value out float 0.15

ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick

# ---- read input, then drive, in ONE exec chain ---------------------------------------------------
# MoveAxis and MouseDelta both carry exec pins for a reason worth knowing: each reads a frame's input
# in a single native call and caches the result for the visit, so reading three of MouseDelta's pins
# costs one call rather than three. That guarantee only holds when the node is REACHED BY THE EXEC
# WALK -- a node hanging off a data link alone is refused rather than sampled at an undefined moment.
NODE move MoveAxis
NODE mouse MouseDelta
LINK tick.exec move.exec
LINK move.then mouse.exec

NODE yaw Multiply
LINK mouse.deltaX yaw.a
LINK look_scale.value yaw.b
NODE pitch Multiply
LINK mouse.deltaY pitch.a
LINK look_scale.value pitch.b

# CharacterMove is deliberately COARSE -- one node, not a kit of primitives -- for the same reason
# Blueprints' own AddMovementInput is: the pitch clamp, the view entity and the capsule stay in one
# tested place instead of becoming every graph author's problem to reassemble correctly.
NODE drive CharacterMove
LINK mouse.then drive.exec
LINK ent.value drive.entity
LINK dt.value drive.dt
LINK move.forward drive.forward
LINK move.right drive.right
LINK yaw.result drive.yawDelta
LINK pitch.result drive.pitchDelta

# ---- reach another entity's graph ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The player spawns one target of its own at OnStart and remembers its entity id, then fires OnHit at
# it every tick. Spawn-and-remember is the ONLY way to obtain a target id today: Raycast's `entity`
# output is a PHYSICS BODY handle and no body-to-entity mapping exists anywhere in the engine, so a
# graph can learn THAT it hit something but not WHAT. That is the one gap still standing between this
# and graph-driven shooting, and it is named here rather than papered over.
# INT here, where Target.ocgraph's counter had to be FLOAT -- an entity id is an Int and
# Spawn.entity is an Int pin, while Add only takes Floats. There is no conversion node, so a
# VAR's type must match whatever consumes it exactly. Both mismatches were caught at LOAD
# with the offending link named, which is the right time to find out.
VAR victim int 0

ENTRY seed OnStart
NODE seed OnStart

NODE sx ConstFloat
PIN sx value out float 250.0
NODE sy ConstFloat
PIN sy value out float 600.0
NODE sz ConstFloat
PIN sz value out float 60.0

NODE make Spawn class=AN_Target
LINK seed.exec make.exec
LINK sx.value make.x
LINK sy.value make.y
LINK sz.value make.z

NODE remember SetVar var=victim
LINK make.then remember.exec
LINK make.entity remember.value

NODE who GetVar var=victim
NODE poke FireEvent event=OnHit
LINK drive.then poke.exec
LINK who.value poke.target

# NO OUT RECORDS, and this one is worth understanding before you write your own multi-entry graph.
# OUT is a GRAPH-WIDE list, not a per-entry one, so EVERY entry point's compiled delegate pulls every
# OUT record. `OUT drive success` was fine while OnTick was the only entry -- adding the OnStart entry
# above made OnStart's own delegate try to pull `drive`, a CharacterMove sitting on OnTick's chain,
# and side-effecting nodes are refused when pulled as data:
#   'drive.success' cannot be read as a data value: CharacterMove has a side effect and must be
#   reached by wiring it directly into the exec chain
# That refusal is correct -- pulling could run the write more than once, or not at all. The lesson is
# that OUT belongs to graphs with ONE entry, or to nodes on no exec chain at all. `success` and
# `fired` are still checked; they simply are not reported, and nothing needs them to be.
